\begin{abstract}
Neuro-symbolic methods propose extracting first-order logic (FOL) constraints from Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning traces to improve answer aggregation beyond majority voting. Whether the reasoning model itself can produce useful constraints through self-extraction has not been systematically evaluated. If self-extraction works, it eliminates the need for trained verifiers, external provers, or domain-specific infrastructure. We evaluate Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA), which formulates a weighted partial Maximum Satisfiability (MAX-SAT) problem over self-extracted FOL constraints, across five model families (7B to 32B parameters) and three reasoning domains with more than 1,300 total instances. We include an oracle control that replaces self-extraction with gold FOL annotations verified by Z3 to isolate extraction quality from aggregation effectiveness. Self-extracted SICA provides no statistically significant improvement over Self-Consistency (SC) in any tested condition. The oracle variant achieves $+8.82$ percentage points over SC ($p < 0.001$), which proves the aggregation mechanism is sound when supplied with high-quality constraints. We identify this failure as a \emph{capability trap}: because the same model that produced potentially flawed reasoning also extracts the constraints, those constraints are semantically coupled to their source answers and carry no independent discriminative signal. Only 11.5\% of self-extracted constraints evaluate differently across candidate answers. This result establishes that same-model constraint extraction cannot improve LLM answer aggregation without independent sources of formal knowledge.
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This work presents a systematic evaluation of symbolic constraint aggregation for LLM majority voting across five models, three reasoning domains, and more than 1,300 instances. The central finding is negative: when an LLM extracts first-order logic constraints from its own reasoning traces and a MAX-SAT solver aggregates them, accuracy does not improve over standard majority voting. The gap ranges from $-3.3$ to $+3.3$ percentage points, with no condition reaching statistical significance. We trace this failure to a capability trap: self-extracted constraints are semantically coupled to the answers they accompany and carry no independent discriminative signal. The MAX-SAT solver agrees with simple constraint counting on 803 of 804 instances, which confirms that satisfiability structure adds nothing when constraints are redundant with votes.

An oracle control experiment isolates constraint quality as the sole bottleneck. Replacing self-extracted constraints with gold FOL annotations yields $+8.82$ percentage points over majority voting ($p < 0.001$), while all other pipeline components remain fixed. The aggregation mechanism is sound; the problem lies entirely in same-model self-extraction. This result quantifies an $8.82$-point gap that remains recoverable through higher-quality constraint sources.

For practitioners, we identify the multi-candidate rate $C_0$ as a zero-cost diagnostic: when $C_0$ falls below 5\%, constraint-based methods cannot improve over majority voting regardless of constraint quality, and investment in extraction is unwarranted. For the field, our results suggest that productive directions include trained FOL extractors, external theorem provers, and cross-model constraint extraction, all of which break the circular dependency between generation and verification that defines the capability trap.

